#X text 701 -695 STOP-TAG FIX (2026-08-20) -- bridge_slicer's outlet 0 carries the untagged play-trigger list (stem startFrac endFrac ratio durMs ...) AND five already-tagged commands sharing the same outlet ("stop" \, "resume" \, "preload" \, "rescheduleLive" \, "resumeSeek" -- see slicer_bridge.js's outlet(0\\\, ...) calls). [list prepend status play] below (idx 160) used to receive ALL of it unconditionally \, so a bare "stop" became "status play stop" -- key="play" \, args=["stop"] once gui_hub_bridge.js's TELEMETRY.status splits it \, which gnumbat-live.js's status handler discards ("stop" is not a valid stem name) instead of ever nulling `playing[r]`. That is why the browser playhead never stopped: the WS frame the client needed ("status stop") never existed. This [route] intercepts the five known tags BEFORE they reach the "status play" prepend and re-tags each with its own real selector instead \, leaving the untagged play-trigger list (route's reject outlet) on the exact path it always used.;
#X text 1357 -1285 SELECT-VS-ROUTE FIX (2026-08-20) -- idx 52 was [select need_stemDurs]\\\, a SINGLE-argument select. Diagnostic taps (now removed -- their job is done) proved bridge_slicer's outlet 1 delivers sourceTrack/sysMsg/ready/track_name/need_stemDurs perfectly intact\\\; the raw tap printed every one of them cleanly while the SAME message crashed [select need_stemDurs] with "no method for 'X'" before it ever reached either of its outlets -- including on its own literal match argument "need_stemDurs". Root cause: a single-arg [select] only accepts float/symbol/bang on its inlet\\\, NOT an arbitrary custom message selector -- unlike [route] (or [select] with 2+ arguments)\\\, which uses full generic dispatch and handles any selector fine. That is the whole difference between this object erroring and idx 61's [route sourceTrack resetMemory ...] in buffer_manager.pd handling the exact same firehose without incident. Fix: swap the object to [route need_stemDurs] -- same argument\\\, same 2-outlet shape (0 = match\\\, 1 = reject/passthrough)\\\, but route's generic dispatch accepts the message instead of erroring on it.;
